## Project structure

```
little-horizons-erp/
├── docs/database.sql
├── config.example.txt
├── pom.xml
└── src/main/java/com/littlehorizons/erp/
    ├── Main.java
    ├── model/
    ├── view/
    ├── controller/
    ├── dao/
    ├── exception/
    └── util/
```

**MVC.** The user only talks to the **view**; the view calls a **controller**; the controller applies business rules and uses a **DAO** to reach the database; data travels in **model** objects.

| Package | Responsibility |
|---------|----------------|
| `model` | Domain entities (classes, records and enums such as `Student`, `Charge`, `Invoice`, `PaymentMethod`). No logic of I/O. |
| `view` | Console menus and input prompts (`StudentView`, `FinancialView`, `ReportView`...). They never contain SQL or business rules. |
| `controller` | Business rules and validation (`PaymentController`, `LoanController`...). They throw `BusinessException` when a rule is broken. |
| `dao` | Data access. One interface per aggregate (`StudentDao`) and one JDBC implementation (`JdbcStudentDao`). All SQL lives here. |
| `exception` | `BusinessException` (checked, rule rejected) and `DataAccessException` (unchecked, wraps `SQLException`). |
| `util` | Shared helpers: `DatabaseConnection`, `ErrorLogger`, `InputValidator`, `Validation`, `Money`, `CufeGenerator`, `InvoiceFileWriter`. |

The extra `dao/` and `exception/` packages are allowed by the project statement (internal packages may be added while respecting MVC).

## Design principles and patterns

### SOLID, with examples from this code

- **S - Single Responsibility.** Each class has one reason to change. `InvoiceFileWriter` only writes the invoice text file; `CufeGenerator` only computes the CUFE hash; `InvoiceController` builds the invoice (range check, VAT, CUFE) and `JdbcInvoiceDao` only stores it.
- **O - Open/Closed.** Behaviour is extended by adding values or classes, not by editing consumers. Adding a new payment method means adding one constant to the `PaymentMethod` enum (it carries its own `dbValue` and `fromDb`); the DAOs and views that list `PaymentMethod.values()` keep working unchanged.
- **L - Liskov Substitution.** Any `JdbcXxxDao` can replace its interface without the controller noticing: `PaymentController` only calls `PaymentDao` methods, so a test double or another database implementation can be injected with the same behaviour contract.
- **I - Interface Segregation.** DAO interfaces are small and specific (`GradeDao`, `GuardianDao`, `StudentDao`, `ReportDao`...) instead of one giant `Dao`. `ReportController` depends only on the read-only `ReportDao` and cannot modify data.
- **D - Dependency Inversion.** Controllers depend on DAO **interfaces**, not on JDBC classes. The concrete objects are created in one place, the composition root `Main`, and injected through constructors, e.g. `new PaymentController(paymentDao, invoiceController, studentDao, guardianDao)`.

### Design patterns

| Pattern | Where | Why |
|---------|-------|-----|
| **Singleton** | `DatabaseConnection` | One place reads `config.txt` and opens connections; the configuration is loaded once. |
| **DAO** | `dao/` package | Isolates SQL from business rules and lets the database be replaced without touching controllers. |
| **MVC** | `model` / `view` / `controller` | Separates console interaction, business rules and data. |
| **Dependency Injection (manual)** | `Main` | Wires views, controllers and DAOs without a framework. |

### Other technical practices

- `PreparedStatement` in every query (no string concatenation of user input).
- `try-with-resources` for every `Connection`, `PreparedStatement` and `ResultSet`.
- Lambdas and Streams in controllers (`ReportController`, `PaymentController`) and views.
- Transactions (`commit`/`rollback`) for multi-table operations: student plus guardian registration, payment plus invoice, loans. `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` protects club capacity and resource availability.
- `BigDecimal` for money and grades.

## Main features

**Students and guardians.** Register, search, update and withdraw students; at least one guardian per student (saved in one transaction); document type consistent with age (RC up to 7, TI from 7, CC from 18).

**Teachers.** Register and manage teachers, subject assignments and weekly schedule; conflict detection for teachers and groups, school hours 06:00 to 17:00, weekly hour limit.

**Academic.** Grades from 0.0 to 5.0 with one decimal per period, subject and period averages, students needing support (grade under 3.0), attendance records and weekly absence alerts (more than 3).

**Extracurricular.** Clubs with maximum capacity checked inside a transaction, memberships; events with participants, roles and unique podium positions (events only accept participants once they are past or today).

**Financial.** Payment concept catalog, charges per student with percentage discount (with required reason), multi-concept payments (one invoice per payment), receipts `REC-YYYY-NNNNNN`, payment history by student and by guardian, debtors report (pending for more than one month), electronic-style invoice as a `.txt` file with simulated CUFE (SHA-384) and QR content.

**Resources.** Inventory by category and status; loans for students (limit 3) and teachers (limit 5) in separate tables; 1 to 30 days; no new loan if the borrower has an overdue one; overdue loans are marked automatically.

**Reports and statistics.** General summary, top students, students at academic risk, average by grade, attendance by grade, club occupancy, monthly income and inventory status.

## Error handling and logs

- **Log file:** `error_log.txt`, created in the folder where the application runs (project root). It is plain text, written by `ErrorLogger` without any logging library. It is ignored by Git.
- **Format:** `[yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss] [LEVEL] message`.
- **Levels:**
  - `INFO`: application events (application start and finish).
  - `WARN`: every rejected user input or broken business rule, with the prompt and the reason (the typed value is not stored).
  - `ERROR`: technical failures such as SQL errors, with the exception.
- **Strategy:** DAOs catch `SQLException`, log an `ERROR` and throw `DataAccessException` (unchecked) with a friendly message; controllers throw `BusinessException` (checked) for rule violations; views catch both and show a clear `[ERROR]` message. The application never shows a stack trace to the user.

Sample lines:

```
[2026-11-17 14:08:31] [INFO] Application started
[2026-11-17 14:08:32] [WARN] Invalid input at 'Valor base en pesos:': Ingrese un número válido.
[2026-11-17 14:08:38] [WARN] Invalid input at 'Seleccione una opción:': Ingrese un número entre 0 y 10.
[2026-11-17 14:09:10] [ERROR] Database connection test failed | java.sql.SQLException: ...
[2026-11-17 14:09:15] [INFO] Application finished
```

